fkJ","description":"Read the title lazy bones","caption":"grizzly creek fire","poi_name":null,"subject":"grizzly creek fire","author":"JimmyElsen58","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2021-05-22T18:51:00.000Z","updated_at":"2021-05-23T00:51:43.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/pobEyYI5OcO24UgPIIfkJ","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/pobEyYI5OcO24UgPIIfkJ"}},{"id":"8502","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7759","description":"This is Barrel Springs Rapid at ultra low water.  I went back up on the 28th and it was even lower.","caption":"Barrel Springs","poi_name":"Barrel Springs","subject":"","author":"Kit Davidson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8502","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8502"}},{"id":"tEnTRV3x73gM53fC7wVWY","ext":"jpg","post_id":"tEnTRV3x73gM53fC7wVWY","description":"Power plant is in play as water is diverted \nThis is the white room hole first drop after life after death","caption":"3500 @ Dotsero ","poi_name":null,"subject":"3500 @ Dotsero ","author":"JimmyElsen58","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2021-05-02T14:20:00.000Z","updated_at":"2021-05-02T20:21:58.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/tEnTRV3x73gM53fC7wVWY","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/tEnTRV3x73gM53fC7wVWY"}},{"id":"8876","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7760","description":"Close up of upper part of Upper Death.","caption":"Upper Death (3)","poi_name":"Upper Death","subject":"","author":"Kit Davidson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8876","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8876"}},{"id":"8882","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7760","description":"This is the hole at the bottom of Shoshone Dam.  Ouch.","caption":"Hole At Bottom Of Dam","poi_name":"n/a","subject":"","author":"Kit Davidson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8882","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8882"}},{"id":"8877","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7760","description":"A good shot of Upper Death.","caption":"Upper Death (4)","poi_name":"Upper Death","subject":"","author":"Kit Davidson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8877","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8877"}},{"id":"8878","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7760","description":"Same shot as Upper Death (1) but with more water.","caption":"Upper Death (5)","poi_name":"Upper Death","subject":"","author":"Kit Davidson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8878","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8878"}},{"id":"8875","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7760","description":"This is a scary looking hole at the bottom of Upper Death.","caption":"Scary Hole","poi_name":"Upper Death","subject":"","author":"Kit Davidson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8875","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8875"}},{"id":"8503","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7759","description":"Upper Death at ultra-low water.  This rapid surprised the hell out of me on how steep it is.","caption":"Upper Death (1)","poi_name":"Upper Death","subject":"","author":"Kit Davidson","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8503","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8503"}},{"id":"CvDn7kzQe80YJu0f8C4yR","ext":"jpg","post_id":"CvDn7kzQe80YJu0f8C4yR","description":"Skid","caption":"stomp","poi_name":null,"subject":"stomp","author":"JimmyElsen58","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2021-05-22T18:53:00.000Z","updated_at":"2021-05-23T00:53:50.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/CvDn7kzQe80YJu0f8C4yR","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/CvDn7kzQe80YJu0f8C4yR"}}],"detail":{"id":"380","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"This is the steepest section of the Colorado River and the hardest whitewater in Glenwood Canyon. The run changes significantly based on flows, so make sure to check the level before you head out. There are 3 major rapids within this section: Upper Death, Lower Death, and Barrell springs. From Hanging Lake, hike down the bike path from the parking lot and you will see Upper Death. Upper Death is only runnable at high flows and most people will want to walk this rapid due to the massive, steep, terrifying nature of the drop. If you do decide you want to run Upper death, you can scout it in its entirety from the bike path and put in just below the dam.\n\nMost people put in below Upper Death which marks the beginning of Lower Death, a fairly straightforward class IV rapid. Just run down the middle after you peel out of the put-in eddy and run to the right of the massive boulder on river left. After Lower Death, there is a long section of flatwater and once you see a wooden handrail on the river right bike path, you will know you have reached Barrel. Eddy out on river right and scout from the bike path. This rapid heavily changes character based on flows. Below 2000 CFS the run is class IV creeking. Between 2,000 and 3,000 CFS Barrel is IV/IV+ river running and above 3,000 CFS the run turns into class V big water. Barrel is generally divided into 3 different dops, the crux being the middle drop next to a large boulder on river left that resembles Africa dubbed \"Africa Rock\". The bottom drop is the easiest and at proper flows has an amazing boof at the bottom. Make sure there's enough water because at lower flows it turns into an aggressive piton. After you finish up Barrell some read and run will lead you to the put-in for Shoshone. Most people set the shuttle at Grizzly Creek and paddle through Shoshone to end the lap with some nice class III.\n\nACCESS: The traditional put in for Barrell is the Hanging Lake Exit in Glenwood canyon, however, due to increased levels of tourism through recent years the parking lot access has been shut down to hikers, and in turn boaters, so that is no longer an option. The new norm for running this section is to park at the Shoshone exit and hike up to Barrell Springs. Shuttle Can either be run from the Shoshone exit or from Grizzly Creek if you want to paddle through Shoshone.\n\nReaches of the Colorado River:\n\n[01\\. Hot Sulphur Springs to Hwy 40 bridge (Byers Canyon)](../../content/River_detail_id_377_) (CO, IV)\n\n[02\\. Gore Canyon](../../content/River_detail_id_378_) (CO, IV-V)\n\n[03\\. Pumphouse campground to Rancho Del Rio (Pumphouse)](../../content/River_detail_id_379_) (CO, III)\n\n[04\\. Hanging Lake Exit 125 (I-70) to Shoshone Power Plant Exit 123 (I-70) (Barrel Springs)](../../content/River_detail_id_380_) (CO, IV-V \\[V+\\])\n\n[05\\. Shoshone Power Plant, Exit 123 (I-70) to Grizzly Creek, Exit 121 (I-70) (Shoshone)](../../content/River_detail_id_4260_) (CO, III-IV)\n\n[06\\. Cameo Dam (Big Sur / Lucky 7)](../../content/River_detail_id_4261_) (CO, III)\n\n[07\\. Loma to Westwater (Ruby / Horsethief Canyons)](../../content/River_detail_id_381_) (CO-UT, II)\n\n[08\\. Westwater to Rose Ranch (Westwater Canyon)](../../content/River_detail_id_1840_) (UT, I-IV)\n\n[09\\. Cisco (Rose Ranch) to Moab (Professor Valley)](../../content/River_detail_id_1841_) (UT, I-III)\n\n[10\\. Moab to Powell Reservoir (Cataract Canyon)](../../content/River_detail_id_1842_) (UT, I-IV)\n\n[11\\. Lees Ferry to Lake Mead (Grand Canyon)](../../content/River_detail_id_114_) (AZ, I-V)\n\n[12\\. Black Canyon](../../content/River_detail_id_3190_) (AZ-NV, I)","averageGradient":"83","maxGradient":null,"length":"2.5","correlations":[{"reachID":"380","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":"The optimal flow for this reach is 1900 (Flows based on 2012 WFET Basin Report). ","highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"900","beginMediumRunnable":"1800","beginHighRunnable":"2000","endHighRunnable":"5000"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"COLORADO RIVER NEAR DOTSERO, CO","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"09070500","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"863","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"2.2","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"39.6446111","longitude":"-107.0780139"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/09070500","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/09070500/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":"Previous Flowbands: \nRange of Boatable Flows (R0-R9): 900 - 5000 cfs  Comment: 900 is the minimum acceptable flow and there is no maximum suggested flow (Flows based on 2012 WFET Basin Report). \nMedium Runnable (R4-R6): 1800 - 2000 cfs  Comment: The optimal flow for this reach is 1900 (Flows based on 2012 WFET Basin Report). \n\nMigration Errors: \nThere are comments on ranges that cannot be migrated","comment":null,"status":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"863","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null}}],"pointOfInterestIDs":["111325","116593"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-107.19395993481376,39.587419788138995],[-107.19406827738703,39.587365671885834],[